//# #2745: a shape recorded inside an absorbed block does not outlive it.
//
// The other direction of the absorb walk's shape recording. A binder's
// classification was already dropped at the end of its block; its SHAPE has to
// be dropped with it, or an inner `let p = (7, 1)` would still be what a later
// read of the OUTER `p` pairs against -- and this file, whose outer `p` holds
// the erased formal, would compile and print the representation.
//
// The keys a binding owns are `<family>$<name>` and `<family>$<name>$<slot>`
// across the four families, so the same prefix test that copies them for an
// alias also identifies them for the scope compaction. One statement of what a
// binding owns, read by both.
//
// RED-TESTED, because this one cannot be red-tested against an older compiler:
// before the absorb walk recorded shapes at all there was nothing for the
// compaction to drop, so every compiler prior to 7101f3e refuses this file for
// a reason that is not the one under test. The mutation is the guard itself --
// `dtd_interp_key_owned_by(names, e.0)` ANDed with false at its call site in
// `dtd_interp_absorb_scoped_bound` -- built through stage2 and measured: the
// file COMPILES and `_start` returns 3, a 3-digit pointer decimal. A fixture
// that passes either way is not a guard, and this one was one line of reasoning
// away from being exactly that.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    "\{ { let p = (a, 1)
    let mut y = 0
    { { let p = (7, 1)
      y = 0
      0 }
      0 }
    match p {
      (b, _) => b
    } } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
